export const DATA_44: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-052", num: 52, title: "Đề 052", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Benefits of Community Gardening", paragraphs: ["In cities and towns around the world, community gardens have become increasingly popular over the past few decades. A community garden is a shared piece of land where local residents come together to grow vegetables, fruits, flowers, and herbs. Unlike private gardens, which belong to a single household, community gardens are open to everyone in the neighbourhood. People of all ages and backgrounds can participate, and the plots of land are usually managed collectively or divided into individual sections that members tend themselves. These green spaces offer a wide range of benefits that go far beyond simply producing food.", "One of the most important advantages of community gardening is the positive effect it has on people's physical health. Working in a garden involves regular physical activity such as digging, planting, watering, and weeding. These activities provide a moderate form of exercise that is suitable for people of different fitness levels, including older adults and those who find more intense forms of exercise difficult. Studies have shown that people who garden regularly tend to eat more fresh vegetables and fruits, partly because they grow them themselves and have easy access to produce that is both nutritious and free of harmful pesticides.", "Community gardens also play an important role in improving mental health. Spending time in green spaces and working with soil has been shown to reduce levels of stress and anxiety. Many people find that gardening gives them a sense of calm and focus that is difficult to achieve in a busy urban environment. For those who live alone or feel socially isolated, the community garden can serve as a welcoming social space where they can meet neighbours, make friends, and feel part of something larger than themselves. Psychologists have noted that having a regular routine and a sense of purpose — both of which gardening provides — are closely linked to better overall wellbeing.", "Beyond individual health, community gardens contribute to the environmental health of cities. Urban areas are often characterised by large amounts of concrete and asphalt, which absorb heat and contribute to what is known as the urban heat island effect. Plants and trees help to mitigate this effect by providing shade and releasing moisture into the air through a process called transpiration. Community gardens also support biodiversity by providing habitat for bees, butterflies, and other pollinators that are essential for healthy ecosystems. In cities where green space is limited, even a small garden can make a meaningful difference to local wildlife.", "Community gardens can also have a positive impact on the social fabric of a neighbourhood. When people work together towards a shared goal, they develop a sense of trust and cooperation that can extend beyond the garden itself. Research has found that neighbourhoods with active community gardens tend to have stronger social connections and lower levels of crime than those without. Gardens can also serve as educational spaces where children learn about nature, food production, and the environment in a hands-on way that cannot easily be replicated in a classroom. Some community gardens run dedicated programmes for local schools, inviting children to plant seeds, observe growth, and harvest crops across the school year.", "Despite their many benefits, community gardens do face certain challenges. Securing land in dense urban areas can be difficult, and gardens are sometimes threatened by property developers who wish to build on the land. Funding for tools, seeds, and infrastructure such as water access and fencing can also be a problem, particularly for gardens run entirely by volunteers. However, many cities have recognised the value of community gardens and have introduced policies to protect existing green spaces and support the creation of new ones. With the right backing from local governments and communities, these shared spaces have the potential to become lasting features of urban life."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["How to start and manage a successful community garden in an urban area", "The difficulties that community gardens face in cities where land is expensive and scarce", "Why governments should invest more money in public green spaces and parks", "The wide range of physical, mental, environmental, and social benefits that community gardens provide"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following physical activities is involved in community gardening?", options: ["Swimming and running", "Lifting and building garden structures", "Digging, planting, watering, and weeding", "Carrying heavy equipment across long distances"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how can community gardens benefit people who feel socially isolated?", options: ["By providing a structured daily exercise programme led by professional instructors", "By offering a social space where they can meet neighbours and feel part of a community", "By giving them a private area to spend time alone away from the pressures of urban life", "By connecting them with mental health professionals who volunteer in the garden"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do plants in community gardens help reduce the urban heat island effect?", options: ["By generating cool air through photosynthesis that spreads to surrounding streets", "By blocking sunlight from reaching the ground and keeping temperatures low underground", "By absorbing large amounts of concrete and asphalt that trap heat in city surfaces", "By providing shade and releasing moisture into the air through transpiration"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"mitigate\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["worsen", "explain", "reduce", "measure"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as a challenge facing community gardens?", options: ["A lack of interest from younger generations in learning how to grow food", "Disagreements between garden members about how to divide the available land", "Difficulties in persuading local residents to donate time to volunteer programmes", "The threat posed by property developers who want to build on garden land"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["community gardens located near residential areas", "harmful pesticides found in commercial produce", "fresh vegetables and fruits", "people who garden regularly"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"replicated\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["fully understood", "improved upon", "reproduced in the same way", "replaced by something better"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a social benefit of community gardens?", options: ["Offering free legal advice to residents who face disputes over land use", "Providing hands-on nature education for local children", "Strengthening trust and cooperation among neighbourhood residents", "Helping reduce levels of crime in the surrounding area"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 6 about the future of community gardens in cities?", options: ["The main obstacle facing community gardens is the lack of experienced gardeners to manage shared land", "Support from local governments is a key factor in determining whether community gardens can survive and expand", "Community gardens will eventually disappear as cities continue to grow and land becomes more valuable", "Property developers and community gardeners are increasingly working together to find mutually beneficial solutions"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Understanding Inertia: The Law of Motion", paragraphs: ["Of all the principles that form the foundation of classical physics, few are as elegantly simple and as far-reaching in their consequences as Newton's First Law of Motion, commonly known as the law of inertia. First formally stated by the English mathematician and physicist Isaac Newton in his landmark work Principia Mathematica, published in 1687, the law declares that an object at rest will remain at rest, and an object in motion will continue moving in a straight line at a constant speed, unless acted upon by an external force. This statement, deceptively straightforward on its surface, fundamentally reshaped how scientists and philosophers understood the natural world and challenged centuries of received wisdom about the nature of movement itself.", "Before Newton articulated his laws, the dominant view of motion in Western science had been shaped largely by the ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle, who believed that all moving objects naturally tend to slow down and come to rest unless a force is continuously applied to keep them moving. According to Aristotle, rest was the natural state of objects, and motion required constant effort to sustain. This intuition seems to be confirmed by everyday experience: a ball rolled across the ground slows down and stops, a cart pushed along a road decelerates once the pushing ceases. The problem with Aristotle's explanation, which took over a thousand years for scientists to fully recognise, was that it failed to account for the hidden force of friction — the resistance generated when surfaces in contact move against one another.", "It was the Italian astronomer and physicist Galileo Galilei who, in the early seventeenth century, first conducted experiments that began to undermine the Aristotelian view. Galileo observed that when a ball rolled down a smooth inclined surface onto a horizontal plane, it continued rolling for a much greater distance than Aristotle's framework would predict. He reasoned that if friction could be entirely eliminated — a condition that cannot be achieved in the real world but can be approached through careful experimental design — a rolling ball would never stop at all. This insight, known as Galileo's principle of inertia, was the direct predecessor of Newton's First Law and represented one of the most significant conceptual breakthroughs in the history of science.", "The concept of inertia has a precise meaning in physics that is distinct from its casual use in everyday language. In physics, inertia refers specifically to the tendency of any object to resist changes to its state of motion. An object that is stationary resists being set in motion; an object that is moving resists being slowed, stopped, or redirected. The magnitude of an object's inertia is directly proportional to its mass: a heavily loaded freight train is far harder to accelerate from rest or bring to a stop than a bicycle, not because the train has more powerful brakes or a stronger engine, but simply because it has far greater mass and therefore far greater inertia. This relationship between mass and inertia is one of the most fundamental in all of classical mechanics.", "The practical implications of inertia are visible in countless aspects of daily life, often in situations where people do not consciously think of the underlying physics. When a car brakes suddenly, passengers lurch forward because their bodies, in accordance with the law of inertia, continue moving in the direction they were travelling even after the vehicle has stopped. Seat belts and airbags are engineering solutions designed specifically to manage the consequences of inertia in road collisions by providing an external force — contact pressure — that decelerates the passenger's body at a rate survivable by human tissue. Similarly, when a tablecloth is pulled quickly from beneath a set of dishes, the dishes tend to remain in place because their inertia resists the brief horizontal force applied to the cloth beneath them.", "Beyond its applications in engineering and everyday safety, inertia plays a central role in understanding the motion of planets, moons, and other celestial bodies. In the vacuum of space, where friction is essentially absent, the law of inertia operates in its purest form. The planets of our solar system continue to orbit the Sun not because any force is actively pushing them along their paths, but because their inertia causes them to resist any deviation from their current state of motion, while gravity continuously deflects that motion into a curved orbital path rather than allowing it to proceed in a straight line. Without gravity, inertia alone would cause every planet to fly off into space in a straight line; without inertia, gravity would pull every planet directly into the Sun.", "Newton's First Law, for all its simplicity, thus contains within it one of the deepest insights in the history of science: that the natural state of the universe is not rest, but motion — and that what requires explanation is not why things move, but why they stop. This inversion of the intuitive Aristotelian worldview opened the door to a systematic understanding of mechanics, gravitation, and ultimately the physical laws that govern everything from the trajectory of a thrown ball to the orbit of distant galaxies."], questions: [{ q: "What is the primary purpose of the passage?", options: ["To explain the concept of inertia, trace its historical development, and explore its scientific and practical significance", "To describe the specific experiments that Isaac Newton conducted to prove his First Law of Motion", "To argue that Newton's contributions to physics were more important than those of Galileo or Aristotle", "To compare the different ways in which inertia affects objects on Earth versus objects in outer space"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the central flaw in Aristotle's explanation of motion?", options: ["He assumed that all objects in space behave in the same way as objects on Earth", "He failed to account for the role of friction as a hidden force that slows moving objects", "He refused to conduct experiments, relying instead entirely on abstract philosophical reasoning", "He incorrectly believed that heavier objects fall faster than lighter ones in all circumstances"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what conclusion did Galileo draw from his experiments with a ball on a smooth inclined surface?", options: ["That Aristotle's framework was essentially correct but needed minor adjustments to account for surface roughness", "That friction is impossible to reduce below a certain minimum level in any real experiment", "That in the complete absence of friction, a rolling object would continue moving indefinitely", "That heavier balls travel further than lighter balls on a smooth surface"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is a loaded freight train harder to stop than a bicycle?", options: ["Because the train operates on smooth metal rails that provide less friction than a road surface", "Because the train's braking system is less efficient than a bicycle's at converting speed into stopping force", "Because the train travels at much higher speeds, which makes any braking force less effective", "Because the train has far greater mass, which gives it far greater inertia and resistance to changes in motion"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do passengers lurch forward when a car brakes suddenly?", options: ["Because the braking force of the car pushes the passengers toward the front of the vehicle", "Because their bodies continue moving in the original direction of travel due to inertia, even after the car stops", "Because the seat design in most vehicles does not provide sufficient support during deceleration", "Because the airbag inflates and creates a pressure wave that pushes passengers forward"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an example of inertia in everyday life?", options: ["Seat belts and airbags providing force to decelerate passengers in a collision", "A ball continuing to roll across a flat surface after being released", "A tablecloth being pulled from beneath dishes that remain in place", "Passengers leaning forward when a vehicle decelerates rapidly"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"magnitude\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["speed", "direction", "size or degree", "importance"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"deflects\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["increases", "stops completely", "slows down gradually", "redirects at an angle"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["surfaces that generate friction when objects move across them", "ancient Greek philosophers who studied natural motion", "moving objects that tend to slow down according to Aristotle", "external forces acting on stationary objects"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What does the final paragraph suggest is the deepest insight contained in Newton's First Law?", options: ["That Newton's achievement was greater than Galileo's because he expressed the law of inertia in precise mathematical terms", "That the natural state of the universe is motion rather than rest, making stillness the phenomenon that requires explanation", "That gravity is the most important force in the universe because it prevents all objects from flying off into space", "That friction is the primary obstacle preventing scientists from observing inertia in its purest form on Earth"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Impact of Artificial Intelligence in Modern Education", paragraphs: ["Artificial intelligence has emerged as one of the most consequential technological developments of the twenty-first century, and its influence is being felt with increasing force across a wide range of sectors. Education is no exception. From intelligent tutoring systems and automated essay grading to personalised learning platforms and AI-generated study materials, the integration of artificial intelligence into educational settings is accelerating rapidly worldwide. Proponents argue that these technologies have the potential to transform schooling in ways that are long overdue, extending high-quality instruction to students who have previously been underserved by conventional educational systems. Critics, however, raise equally serious concerns about equity, data privacy, the displacement of teachers, and the risk of reducing education to a purely transactional exchange of information.", "Among the most widely discussed applications of AI in education is adaptive learning technology. Unlike traditional classroom instruction, in which all students follow the same curriculum at the same pace, adaptive learning platforms use algorithms to continuously assess each student's performance and adjust the difficulty, format, and sequencing of content in real time. A student who demonstrates mastery of a concept is immediately presented with more challenging material; one who struggles receives additional explanations, worked examples, or alternative representations of the same idea. Advocates argue that this approach more closely mirrors the benefits of one-to-one tutoring — long recognised as one of the most effective forms of instruction — and can deliver those benefits to students who would otherwise never have access to a personal tutor.", "Automated assessment is another domain in which AI is making rapid inroads. Natural language processing systems are now capable of evaluating written responses with a degree of consistency and speed that human markers cannot match at scale. In high-stakes testing environments, where large volumes of essays must be scored quickly and reliably, AI grading tools have demonstrated strong correlations with expert human judgement across a range of subjects and question types. [1] Nevertheless, the limitations of these systems are real and should not be minimised. AI markers excel at evaluating structural features — coherence, vocabulary range, grammatical accuracy — but are far less reliable when it comes to assessing originality, nuanced argumentation, or culturally specific references that fall outside their training data. [2]", "The potential of AI to address longstanding inequalities in educational access represents perhaps its most compelling promise. In many low- and middle-income countries, chronic shortages of qualified teachers, inadequate school infrastructure, and vast geographical distances between students and educational institutions place serious constraints on what conventional schooling can deliver. AI-powered platforms that can function on low-cost devices with intermittent internet connectivity offer the possibility of extending basic literacy and numeracy instruction to learners in remote and underserved communities. [3] Several pilot programmes in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia have reported promising results, with students who used AI-assisted learning tools demonstrating measurable gains in foundational skills even in the absence of regular teacher contact. [4]", "Yet the same technology that promises to democratise access to education also carries the risk of deepening existing inequalities if its deployment is not carefully managed. Students from wealthier backgrounds are far more likely to have access to high-specification devices, reliable internet connections, and the kind of digital literacy support that allows them to engage productively with AI learning tools. Those from disadvantaged backgrounds may find themselves presented with technology that they lack either the hardware or the skills to use effectively, resulting in a two-tier educational experience that mirrors and reinforces the socioeconomic divides it was intended to bridge. Critics have also raised concerns about the volume of personal data that AI learning platforms collect, the opacity of the algorithms that process this data, and the potential for these systems to encode and perpetuate the biases present in their training datasets.", "The question of what happens to teachers in an AI-enhanced educational landscape is one that generates considerable anxiety and debate. The most optimistic scenarios cast AI as a tool that liberates teachers from routine administrative tasks — grading, record-keeping, progress monitoring — and allows them to focus on the relational, creative, and critical dimensions of teaching that machines cannot replicate. More pessimistic accounts warn of a gradual erosion of the teaching profession as schools, under financial pressure, come to view AI as a cheaper substitute for human educators rather than a complement to them. The evidence from early adopters suggests a more nuanced reality: in schools where AI has been integrated thoughtfully and with adequate teacher training, the technology tends to augment rather than replace human instruction; where implementation has been hasty or under-resourced, the outcomes are far less encouraging.", "Looking ahead, the trajectory of AI in education will be shaped less by the technology itself than by the choices that governments, institutions, and communities make about how to deploy it. The most important of these choices concern not the technical capabilities of AI systems but the values that guide their application: whether the goal is to maximise efficiency or to deepen understanding; whether the measure of educational success is test score performance or the cultivation of curiosity, critical thinking, and civic participation. These are not questions that AI can answer — they are questions about what education is for, and they demand the kind of human deliberation and democratic accountability that no algorithm, however sophisticated, is equipped to provide."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Artificial intelligence will inevitably replace teachers in most educational systems within the next decade", "The primary benefit of AI in education is its ability to grade large volumes of student work more quickly and consistently than human markers", "AI offers significant opportunities to transform education but also raises serious concerns about equity, privacy, and the role of teachers, with outcomes depending on how societies choose to deploy it", "Governments in low-income countries should prioritise AI-powered learning platforms over traditional school infrastructure investment"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does adaptive learning technology differ from traditional classroom instruction?", options: ["It continuously adjusts content difficulty and format based on each individual student's real-time performance", "It uses fixed curricula designed by AI experts that are updated annually based on national test results", "It replaces subject-specific content with general skills training that applies across all academic disciplines", "It requires students to work independently without any guidance from a teacher or platform"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, in which area do AI marking systems perform least reliably?", options: ["Judging the coherence and logical structure of an argument", "Evaluating originality and culturally specific content outside their training data", "Evaluating the grammatical accuracy of written responses", "Assessing the range and appropriateness of vocabulary used"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did pilot programmes in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia demonstrate?", options: ["That AI learning tools are only effective when students have continuous access to high-speed internet", "That the cost of deploying AI in remote communities remains too high for most developing nations", "That students using AI tools showed measurable improvement in basic skills even without regular teacher contact", "That AI platforms are most beneficial when used as a supplement to full-time qualified teachers"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a risk of AI deployment in education?", options: ["The collection of large amounts of students' personal data by learning platforms", "AI tutoring systems providing incorrect information that misleads students in core subjects", "AI systems encoding and reinforcing biases present in the data used to train them", "Wealthier students having better access to the devices and skills needed to use AI tools effectively"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does evidence from early adopters suggest about AI's actual impact on teaching?", options: ["AI consistently reduces the workload of teachers regardless of how it is implemented in schools", "Schools that have adopted AI tend to hire fewer teachers as they become more confident in the technology", "The financial benefits of AI adoption outweigh the costs of teacher training in most school settings", "Thoughtful implementation with adequate teacher training leads to AI complementing rather than replacing human instruction"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"correlations\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["official validations by an independent authority", "significant improvements over", "close agreements or similarities between two sets of results", "detailed records of testing outcomes over time"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the challenges faced by conventional classroom instruction at scale", "the benefits of one-to-one tutoring described earlier in the sentence", "students who have never had access to digital learning platforms", "adaptive algorithms that adjust content based on student performance"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"erosion\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a slow and gradual weakening or reduction over time", "a rapid expansion driven by financial incentives", "a sudden and complete elimination of something", "a deliberate restructuring carried out by policymakers"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Some major international examining bodies have begun incorporating AI-assisted scoring into their official assessment pipelines, citing gains in both speed and marker consistency.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Seafloor Spreading and Plate Tectonics", paragraphs: ["Few scientific revolutions in the twentieth century were as profound or as unexpected as the one that overturned two thousand years of assumptions about the stability of the Earth's surface. The theory of plate tectonics — the understanding that the Earth's outermost shell is divided into a mosaic of rigid plates that move continuously relative to one another — emerged as a coherent scientific framework only in the 1960s, synthesising decades of previously disconnected observations into a single, unifying explanation for phenomena as diverse as earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, mountain formation, and the distribution of fossils across continents separated by thousands of kilometres of ocean. At the heart of this revolution lay a discovery that had been hiding in plain sight beneath the world's oceans: the process known as seafloor spreading.", "The intellectual groundwork for plate tectonics had been laid decades earlier by the German meteorologist Alfred Wegener, who proposed in 1912 that the continents had once been joined in a single supercontinent he called Pangaea, before gradually drifting apart over millions of years. Wegener's evidence was compelling in outline — the striking geometric fit between the coastlines of South America and Africa, the continuity of rock formations and fossil records across continents now separated by ocean — but his theory was widely rejected by the scientific community of his era because he could not identify a plausible mechanism by which continents, embedded in solid oceanic crust, could physically move. The question of mechanism would remain unanswered for nearly five decades, until oceanographic surveys of the mid-ocean ridges began to reveal the true architecture of the ocean floor.", "Mid-ocean ridges are vast underwater mountain ranges that run through the centres of the world's major ocean basins, forming a continuous system more than 65,000 kilometres in length — the longest mountain chain on Earth. In the 1950s and early 1960s, research vessels equipped with improved sonar technology began mapping the topography of the ocean floor in unprecedented detail, and the data they collected revealed a striking pattern. The rocks closest to the mid-ocean ridges were consistently the youngest, while those progressively further from the ridge axes were progressively older. The ocean floor, it appeared, was not ancient and static, as previously assumed, but was being continuously generated at the ridges and moving outward from them on either side.", "The mechanism responsible for this process was identified as mantle convection. Deep within the Earth, the semi-molten rock of the mantle circulates in slow convective currents driven by heat generated by the decay of radioactive elements in the Earth's interior. Where two convective cells rise and diverge beneath a mid-ocean ridge, the overlying oceanic crust is pulled apart, and magma from the mantle wells up through the gap to form new crustal material. This new material solidifies, becomes part of the seafloor, and is progressively transported away from the ridge by continuing convective motion — a process that has been adding new oceanic crust at an average rate of several centimetres per year for hundreds of millions of years. At the opposing margins of the oceanic plates, where they collide with continental plates, the denser oceanic crust is forced downward in a process known as subduction, descending into the mantle where it is eventually reabsorbed. The ocean floor is thus in a state of continuous renewal: created at ridges, consumed at subduction zones, and never older than approximately 200 million years — a fraction of the Earth's 4.5-billion-year age.", "The confirmation of seafloor spreading came through a particularly elegant piece of evidence: the magnetic striping of the ocean floor. Basaltic rocks contain iron-rich minerals that align with the Earth's prevailing magnetic field as they cool and solidify. Geologists had already established that the Earth's magnetic field reverses its polarity at irregular intervals — a process known as geomagnetic reversal — causing the magnetic north and south poles to swap positions. When oceanographers examined the magnetic signatures of the oceanic crust on either side of a mid-ocean ridge, they found a perfectly symmetrical pattern of alternating magnetic stripes: the record of successive geomagnetic reversals, preserved in rock that had solidified at the ridge and been progressively moved outward over time. This evidence, independent of and consistent with the age-distance data from sonar surveys, provided the decisive demonstration that seafloor spreading was not a hypothesis but a measurable physical reality.", "The implications of seafloor spreading extended far beyond the geology of the ocean floor. By providing the missing mechanism for continental drift, it resolved the central objection to Wegener's theory and enabled the synthesis of plate tectonics as a comprehensive framework. The collision of plates explains the formation of mountain ranges such as the Himalayas, produced when the Indian subcontinent collided with the Eurasian plate over tens of millions of years. The subduction of oceanic plates beneath continental margins generates the volcanic arcs and earthquake belts that ring the Pacific Ocean in what is popularly known as the Ring of Fire. Even the distribution of biological species across now-separated landmasses — a pattern that had puzzled naturalists since the nineteenth century — could at last be explained by the movements of plates that had once joined those landmasses together.", "What the discovery of seafloor spreading ultimately demonstrated was that the Earth is not the static, unchanging body that human intuition and centuries of observation had suggested. It is instead a dynamic system in which the surface is continuously reshaped by forces operating over timescales so vast as to be almost incomprehensible to human experience. The scientific revolution that plate tectonics represented was not merely geological: it was a fundamental shift in how humanity understood its position on a planet that is, in the deepest sense, always in motion."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The discovery of seafloor spreading provided the missing mechanism for plate tectonics, transforming scientific understanding of the Earth's dynamic surface", "Magnetic striping of the ocean floor is the single most important piece of evidence supporting the theory of plate tectonics", "The formation of mid-ocean ridges is the primary driver of all geological activity, including earthquakes and volcanic eruptions", "Alfred Wegener's theory of continental drift was the most important scientific contribution of the twentieth century"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"unifying\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["oversimplifying a complex range of phenomena into a single formula", "rapidly spreading from one scientific discipline to many others simultaneously", "bringing together previously separate observations into a single coherent framework", "challenging and ultimately disproving a long-accepted scientific consensus"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why was Wegener's theory of continental drift rejected by scientists of his era?", options: ["Because the scientific community of his time believed the Earth was far too young for continents to have drifted such vast distances", "Because his fossil and rock formation evidence had been collected using methods that other scientists considered unreliable", "Because the geometric fit between the coastlines of South America and Africa was considered too imprecise to be scientifically meaningful", "Because he was unable to propose a credible physical mechanism by which continents embedded in solid oceanic crust could move"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did the age-distance pattern of ocean floor rocks reveal about the seafloor?", options: ["That the topography of the ocean floor is shaped primarily by erosion from deep-sea ocean currents over millions of years", "That rocks on the ocean floor age at the same rate regardless of their distance from a mid-ocean ridge", "That the ocean floor is being continuously generated at mid-ocean ridges and spreading outward, making rocks near the ridge younger", "That the oldest rocks are found at mid-ocean ridges because they have been exposed to volcanic activity for the longest time"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happens to oceanic crust at subduction zones?", options: ["It is compressed into dense metamorphic rock that forms the foundation of continental shelves", "It merges with the continental crust to create a thicker and more stable geological layer", "It accumulates in thick layers that eventually rise above sea level to form new islands", "It is forced beneath continental plates and gradually reabsorbed into the mantle"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does the magnetic striping of the ocean floor constitute particularly strong evidence for seafloor spreading?", options: ["Because the symmetrical pattern of alternating magnetic stripes on either side of ridges records successive reversals in rock that solidified and spread outward over time", "Because it demonstrates that the ocean floor is older than previously assumed, confirming long-standing geological predictions", "Because the magnetic minerals in basaltic rock are unique to oceanic crust and cannot be found in continental rock formations", "Because it shows that the Earth's magnetic field has always pointed in the same direction throughout geological history"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a phenomenon explained by plate tectonics?", options: ["The formation of the Himalayan mountain range through continental collision", "The periodic reversal of the Earth's magnetic poles at irregular intervals", "The distribution of biological species across continents that were once joined together", "The location of earthquake belts and volcanic arcs around the Pacific Ocean"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"polarity\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the strength or intensity of a magnetic field at a given location", "the frequency at which a magnetic field fluctuates between strong and weak states", "the directional orientation of a magnetic field, defining which end is north or south", "the geographic distance between the Earth's magnetic and geographic poles"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["geologists who study geomagnetic reversals", "iron-rich minerals within basaltic rock", "basaltic rocks on the ocean floor", "oceanographers examining magnetic signatures"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What does the final paragraph suggest about the author's view of the plate tectonics revolution?", options: ["The author is cautious about its significance, noting that many aspects of the theory remain contested within the scientific community", "The author believes its most important legacy is the practical applications it enabled in earthquake prediction and volcanic hazard management", "The author views it as a profound conceptual shift that fundamentally changed how humanity understands the nature and history of the planet itself", "The author regards it as primarily a technical advance in geological measurement with limited implications beyond earth science"], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-112", num: 112, title: "Đề 112", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Staying Calm Around Dogs When You Are Afraid", paragraphs: ["For many people, encountering an unfamiliar dog on the street causes nothing more than mild curiosity, but for those who experience a genuine fear of dogs, even a friendly, leashed animal approaching from a distance can trigger a rush of anxiety. This fear, sometimes called cynophobia when it becomes severe, is more common than many people realize, and it can develop for a variety of reasons, including a frightening encounter during childhood, witnessing someone else being bitten, or simply absorbing anxious reactions from a parent who was nervous around dogs. Understanding practical strategies for managing this fear can make daily life considerably easier, particularly for people living in neighborhoods where dogs are frequently walked off-leash or allowed to roam freely.", "The first and most important strategy involves managing body language, since dogs are remarkably skilled at reading human posture, eye contact, and movement, often more skilled than humans are at reading one another. A person who is frightened naturally tends to freeze, back away quickly, or make sudden defensive movements, all of which a dog may interpret as either an invitation to chase or a sign of unpredictability that warrants a cautious or defensive response. Experts who work with anxious dog owners generally recommend the opposite approach: standing still, avoiding direct eye contact (which dogs can perceive as a challenge), and turning the body slightly sideways rather than facing the animal head-on, since a sideways posture tends to appear less confrontational from the dog's perspective.", "Breathing and internal self-talk play a surprisingly significant role as well, even though they may seem disconnected from how a dog actually behaves. Anxiety raises a person's heart rate and causes shallow, rapid breathing, both of which can be picked up by a dog through scent and subtle behavioral cues that humans rarely notice in themselves. Practicing slow, deliberate breathing when a dog approaches, rather than holding one's breath or breathing rapidly, can help prevent the kind of nervous energy that may inadvertently signal alarm to the animal. Some behavioral therapists who treat dog phobias also encourage clients to mentally rehearse calm scenarios in advance, picturing themselves remaining relaxed and composed during an encounter, since this kind of mental preparation can reduce the intensity of the automatic fear response when a real encounter occurs.", "Gradual exposure, conducted carefully and at a pace the individual can tolerate, remains one of the most effective long-term approaches for reducing dog-related anxiety, according to most clinical psychologists who specialize in treating phobias. This typically begins with looking at calm photographs or videos of dogs, then progresses to observing real dogs from a safe distance, such as through a window or across a park, before eventually involving brief, supervised interactions with a particularly calm, well-trained dog under the guidance of its owner or a professional. Rushing this process, such as forcing oneself into close contact with an unfamiliar dog before feeling ready, often backfires , reinforcing the fear rather than reducing it, since a single bad experience during exposure can undo weeks of gradual progress.", "Practical, everyday precautions can also reduce anxiety for people who must regularly navigate environments where dogs are present. Crossing to the opposite side of the street when a dog approaches, asking an owner whether their dog is friendly before getting closer, and carrying a small umbrella or bag that can be opened to create a visual barrier if a dog becomes aggressive are all reasonable strategies that do not require confronting the fear directly. Many cities and towns also have local ordinances requiring dogs to be leashed in public spaces, and people with severe dog phobias can sometimes request that delivery workers, mail carriers, or neighbors keep their dogs secured during scheduled visits.", "Ultimately, while a fear of dogs can feel isolating, particularly in communities where dog ownership is common and dogs are treated as beloved family members, the fear itself is a recognized and treatable condition rather than something to be ashamed of. With a combination of behavioral strategies, gradual exposure, and, when necessary, professional support, most people find that their anxiety around dogs decreases meaningfully over time, even if it never disappears completely."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["A fear of dogs cannot be managed effectively and requires permanent avoidance of all public spaces.", "Professional therapy is the only effective method for treating a fear of dogs in any individual.", "People who fear dogs can manage their anxiety through a combination of practical behavioral strategies and gradual exposure.", "Dogs are inherently dangerous animals that pose a serious threat to anyone who approaches them."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what body language do experts recommend when a dog approaches?", options: ["Backing away quickly while making direct eye contact to assert dominance over the animal.", "Standing still, avoiding direct eye contact, and turning the body slightly sideways.", "Running toward the nearest building while waving arms to attract the owner's attention.", "Freezing completely in place while staring directly at the dog without moving at all."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why can a person's breathing affect how a dog responds to them?", options: ["Dogs become calmer specifically when they hear a person breathing loudly through their mouth.", "Dogs are trained from birth to respond only to specific breathing patterns taught by professional trainers.", "Dogs can pick up on rapid breathing and elevated heart rate through scent and subtle behavioral cues.", "Dogs cannot sense any physiological changes in humans regardless of how anxious the person feels."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the recommended first step in the gradual exposure process?", options: ["Looking at calm photographs or videos of dogs before progressing to real-life observation.", "Asking a professional to bring several dogs into a small, enclosed room all at once.", "Avoiding all contact with dogs indefinitely without ever attempting any form of exposure.", "Immediately interacting closely with an unfamiliar dog to build confidence as quickly as possible."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a practical precaution for managing dog-related anxiety?", options: ["Asking an owner whether their dog is friendly before getting closer.", "Wearing scent-masking spray to avoid being detected by nearby dogs.", "Carrying a small umbrella or bag to create a visual barrier if needed.", "Crossing to the opposite side of the street when a dog approaches."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"confrontational\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["friendly and welcoming", "financially costly", "challenging or aggressive in manner", "quiet and unnoticeable"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"backfires\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["produces an opposite, undesired effect", "occurs very gradually over time", "remains completely unnoticed", "succeeds as planned"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"this\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the recommendation to breathe slowly when a dog approaches", "the practice of asking an owner whether their dog is friendly", "the process of rushing into close contact with an unfamiliar dog", "the gradual exposure process beginning with photographs and videos"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about rushing the gradual exposure process?", options: ["It has no measurable effect on a person's long-term anxiety levels around dogs.", "It is likely to reinforce the fear rather than reduce it, since a single bad experience can undo prior progress.", "It is recommended by most clinical psychologists as the most effective overall strategy.", "It guarantees faster results than a slow, carefully paced approach to overcoming the fear."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To convince readers that a fear of dogs is irrational and should simply be ignored.", "To argue that all dogs should be banned from public spaces for safety reasons.", "To provide practical guidance and reassurance to people managing a fear of dogs.", "To promote a specific therapy clinic that specializes in treating animal phobias."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Children, Sugar, and the Soft Drink Habit", paragraphs: ["Walk through almost any school cafeteria, sports event, or family gathering, and it quickly becomes apparent how deeply embedded sugary soft drinks have become in the everyday diets of children across much of the world. What was once an occasional treat reserved for birthday parties or special outings has, for many families, become a routine part of daily meals, raising growing concern among pediatricians, nutritionists, and public health officials about the long-term consequences of this shift for an entire generation of young people.", "The most immediate and well-documented consequence of frequent soft drink consumption involves excess calorie intake, since a single can of a typical sugary beverage contains an amount of sugar that can approach or exceed the recommended total daily sugar intake for a child. Unlike calories obtained from solid food, which tend to trigger feelings of fullness that naturally limit how much a person eats afterward, calories consumed in liquid form rarely produce the same satiating effect, meaning that children who drink soda alongside a regular meal often end up consuming significantly more total calories than they otherwise would. Over months and years, this seemingly small daily surplus can accumulate into substantial weight gain, contributing to the rising rates of childhood obesity observed in numerous countries.", "Beyond weight concerns, dental health represents another area where the effects of soft drink consumption become apparent relatively quickly. The combination of sugar and acidity found in most soft drinks creates conditions in the mouth that are particularly favorable to the bacteria responsible for tooth decay, and children who sip sugary drinks slowly throughout the day, rather than consuming them quickly at a single sitting, expose their teeth to this damaging environment for considerably longer periods. Pediatric dentists frequently report that children who regularly consume soft drinks show higher rates of cavities compared to children whose sugar intake comes primarily from occasional treats rather than daily beverages.", "Less widely understood, though increasingly studied, is the relationship between sugary drink consumption and disrupted sleep patterns in children. Many popular soft drinks contain caffeine, sometimes in amounts that parents underestimate, and children, who generally weigh far less than adults, can experience the stimulating effects of caffeine far more intensely relative to their body size. Children who consume caffeinated beverages, even in modest quantities, particularly later in the afternoon or evening, frequently experience measurable disruptions to their ability to fall asleep and to the overall quality of sleep once they do, which can in turn affect mood, concentration, and academic performance during the following school day.", "The economic dimension of this issue also deserves attention, since soft drinks are frequently among the least expensive beverage options available, particularly when purchased in bulk or through promotional discounts at supermarkets. Families operating under tight household budgets may find that sugary drinks represent better apparent value than fresh fruit juice or bottled water, especially in areas where access to clean tap water is unreliable or where fresh produce carries a substantial price premium. This economic reality complicates simplistic public health messaging that merely instructs parents to choose healthier beverages without acknowledging the genuine financial constraints that shape many families' purchasing decisions.", "In response to mounting evidence of harm, several governments around the world have introduced policies specifically targeting sugary drink consumption, ranging from taxes imposed directly on sugar-sweetened beverages to restrictions on how such drinks can be marketed to children through television and online advertising. Early evidence from cities and countries that have implemented sugar taxes suggests these measures can produce measurable reductions in consumption, particularly when the resulting price increase is substantial enough to noticeably affect a family's purchasing decision rather than being negligible .", "Addressing childhood soft drink consumption ultimately requires a combination of individual family choices, school-based nutrition policies, and broader structural interventions such as taxation and advertising restrictions, since no single approach addresses every contributing factor driving current consumption patterns. As research continues to clarify the full scope of long-term health consequences, public health advocates increasingly argue that protecting children's health requires looking beyond individual willpower toward the broader environment shaping what children are encouraged, and able, to drink."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Government taxation alone has completely eliminated childhood soft drink consumption in every country studied.", "Childhood obesity is caused exclusively by soft drink consumption, with no other contributing dietary factors.", "Soft drinks are entirely harmless for children as long as they are consumed alongside a balanced regular meal.", "Frequent soft drink consumption among children carries multiple health and economic consequences that require combined individual and structural solutions."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why might children who drink soda with a meal consume more total calories?", options: ["Soft drinks contain significantly fewer calories than solid food, encouraging children to eat more overall.", "Soft drinks slow down digestion so significantly that children feel hungry again almost immediately.", "Liquid calories rarely produce the same feeling of fullness as calories obtained from solid food.", "Children who drink soda automatically reduce their portion sizes during the rest of the meal."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does sipping soft drinks slowly throughout the day increase the risk of cavities?", options: ["It exposes teeth to a sugar-and-acid environment favorable to decay-causing bacteria for longer periods.", "It causes children to consume significantly larger total quantities of sugar than drinking quickly would.", "It prevents saliva from forming at all, which would otherwise protect teeth from any bacterial growth.", "It causes permanent changes to tooth enamel that occur regardless of how often a child brushes."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are children more affected by caffeine in soft drinks than adults?", options: ["Children have a biological immunity to sugar that makes caffeine's effects more noticeable by comparison.", "Children consume soft drinks exclusively in the morning, when caffeine has the strongest effect.", "Children generally weigh far less than adults, intensifying caffeine's stimulating effects relative to body size.", "Children's bodies are physically incapable of processing any amount of caffeine whatsoever."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a consequence of frequent soft drink consumption in children?", options: ["Disrupted sleep patterns related to caffeine content.", "Higher rates of cavities linked to sugar and acidity.", "Increased risk of weight gain due to excess calorie intake.", "Increased risk of developing food allergies in early childhood."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"accumulate\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["disappear gradually", "decrease suddenly", "remain constant", "build up over time"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"negligible\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["permanently fixed", "financially expensive", "too small to matter", "extremely significant"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"this\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["the price premium associated with fresh produce in certain areas", "the fact that soft drinks are often among the least expensive beverage options", "the unreliable access to clean tap water in some regions", "the policy of taxing sugar-sweetened beverages in several countries"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about families operating under tight household budgets?", options: ["They consistently prioritize fresh fruit juice over soft drinks regardless of relative cost.", "They receive government subsidies specifically designated for purchasing bottled water.", "They are entirely unaware that soft drinks contain any sugar or caffeine whatsoever.", "They may choose soft drinks over healthier options due to genuine financial constraints, not simply poor judgment."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward addressing childhood soft drink consumption, as expressed in the passage?", options: ["Strongly accusatory, blaming parents entirely for failing to make healthier beverage choices for their children.", "Dismissive, suggesting that childhood soft drink consumption is a minor issue not worth public attention.", "Understanding and supportive of comprehensive solutions that go beyond blaming individual family choices.", "Entirely optimistic, claiming that government taxation alone has already resolved the underlying problem."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Bridging the Gap: Educational Reform in Remote Mountain Communities", paragraphs: ["Across many countries with significant mountainous terrain, children growing up in remote highland villages continue to face educational disadvantages that persist despite decades of national policy attention and international development funding. While urban and lowland students increasingly benefit from well-resourced schools, qualified teachers, and reliable internet access, children in isolated mountain communities often attend understaffed schools housed in deteriorating buildings, taught by teachers who themselves received limited training and who frequently request transfers to more accessible postings as soon as institutional rules permit. This persistent disparity raises difficult questions about whether conventional, one-size-fits-all education policy can ever adequately address the distinctive challenges facing geographically isolated communities.", "Geographic isolation itself constitutes perhaps the most fundamental obstacle to educational improvement in mountain regions, since poor road infrastructure can transform what would be a short commute in flatter terrain into an arduous , multi-hour journey, particularly during rainy seasons when unpaved mountain roads frequently become impassable. Many students walk considerable distances simply to reach a school bus stop or boarding facility, and some families, recognizing the physical toll this places on younger children, opt to delay enrollment or withdraw children from school entirely once basic literacy has been achieved, judging the ongoing cost in time and physical strain to outweigh the benefits of continued formal education. Boarding schools have emerged in many regions as a partial solution, allowing students to remain on campus during the school week, though this arrangement introduces its own complications, including extended separation from family and, for younger children especially, considerable emotional adjustment difficulty.", "Teacher retention represents a second persistent challenge that geographic isolation only compounds further. Newly qualified teachers assigned to remote mountain postings frequently view the assignment as a temporary, undesirable obligation rather than a long-term career commitment, leading to high turnover rates that disrupt continuity in students' education and prevent the accumulation of contextual knowledge about local communities that experienced, long-serving teachers typically develop over time. Several governments have experimented with financial incentives, such as housing allowances or salary premiums for educators willing to commit to multi-year postings in remote areas, alongside investments in teacher training programs specifically designed to prepare instructors for the particular pedagogical and cultural challenges of mountain classrooms, including multigrade teaching and instruction across linguistic minority communities whose first language differs from the national language of instruction.", "Language presents a particularly thorny dimension of this broader challenge, since many mountain communities belong to ethnic minority groups whose children arrive at school speaking a language other than the one used for formal instruction and standardized testing. Children forced to learn academic content in an unfamiliar language while simultaneously mastering that language itself face a substantially steeper learning curve than peers who are taught in their native tongue from the outset, a disadvantage that compounds year after year if left unaddressed. Bilingual education programs, which gradually transition students from instruction in their home language toward the national language over several years rather than immersing them immediately, have shown encouraging results in regions where they have been implemented, though scaling such programs across linguistically diverse mountain regions requires substantial investment in specialized curriculum materials and bilingual teacher training that many education ministries have struggled to fund adequately.", "Technology has increasingly been proposed as a partial remedy for some of these structural challenges, with solar-powered tablets, satellite internet connections, and pre-loaded educational software distributed to schools that previously had no reliable access to digital learning resources or even consistent electricity. Early evaluations of these technology-based interventions suggest meaningful but uneven results, since the success of any digital initiative depends heavily on whether teachers receive adequate training to integrate new tools into their existing teaching practice rather than treating devices as an isolated add-on disconnected from the core curriculum. Programs that paired technology distribution with sustained teacher training and ongoing technical support have generally outperformed those that simply distributed devices without comparable investment in human capacity building.", "Community involvement has also proven to be a significant, if sometimes underappreciated, factor in determining whether educational interventions succeed or fail in mountain settings. Programs designed with substantial input from local parents, village leaders, and community organizations tend to achieve higher rates of sustained student attendance and parental support than externally designed interventions imposed without meaningful local consultation, partly because community members possess detailed practical knowledge about which barriers most significantly affect their own children's schooling, knowledge that distant policymakers often lack entirely. Some of the most successful programs have incorporated local community members as paid teaching assistants or liaisons, simultaneously addressing staffing shortages while building lasting local investment in the school's continued success.", "Taken together, the accumulated evidence from mountain education initiatives across diverse countries suggests that no single intervention, whether infrastructure investment, teacher incentives, bilingual curriculum, or technology distribution, can independently resolve the layered disadvantages facing remote highland students. Sustainable improvement appears to depend instead on coordinated, context-sensitive strategies that address geographic, linguistic, professional, and community dimensions simultaneously, an approach that demands more sustained political commitment and funding than the periodic, isolated initiatives that have characterized much mountain education policy to date."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Improving education in remote mountain communities requires coordinated strategies addressing geographic, linguistic, professional, and community challenges together.", "Technology distribution alone has proven sufficient to resolve the educational disadvantages facing mountain communities.", "Mountain communities have rejected all forms of government assistance in favor of entirely self-directed educational programs.", "Geographic isolation is the only meaningful obstacle facing mountain education, and resolving it alone would solve most problems."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do some families delay or withdraw children from school in mountain regions?", options: ["They cannot afford the tuition fees charged by boarding schools in mountain regions.", "They believe formal education provides no meaningful advantage for children living in rural communities.", "They judge the ongoing cost in time and physical strain of commuting to outweigh the benefits of continued schooling.", "They are required by local custom to keep children at home once they reach a certain age."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one consequence of high teacher turnover in mountain schools?", options: ["It eliminates the need for governments to invest in teacher training programs designed for mountain classrooms.", "It reduces the overall cost of running mountain schools by requiring fewer salaried positions.", "It prevents the accumulation of contextual knowledge about local communities that long-serving teachers typically develop.", "It significantly increases the number of students who choose to attend boarding schools instead."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what disadvantage do children face when taught academic content in an unfamiliar language?", options: ["They are automatically placed in lower grade levels regardless of their academic ability.", "They lose the ability to communicate in their native language entirely within a few years.", "They are required to repeat the same grade level multiple times before advancing further.", "They face a steeper learning curve that compounds year after year if left unaddressed."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a strategy for improving mountain education?", options: ["Distributing solar-powered tablets and pre-loaded educational software to schools.", "Offering financial incentives such as housing allowances for teachers in remote postings.", "Implementing bilingual education programs that gradually transition students between languages.", "Relocating entire mountain communities closer to urban centers with better infrastructure."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about technology-based interventions that lack teacher training?", options: ["They are preferred by most teachers over programs involving sustained professional training.", "They have generally underperformed compared to programs that paired technology with sustained teacher training.", "They have proven more effective than any other type of intervention attempted in mountain regions.", "They eliminate the need for any further investment in mountain education infrastructure."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"arduous\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["financially costly", "effortless", "unusually short", "extremely difficult"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"compounds\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["remains unchanged", "disappears suddenly", "worsens progressively", "resolves completely"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"this\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the practice of allowing students to remain on campus during the school week", "the construction of new roads connecting mountain villages to lowland areas", "the requirement that students walk long distances to reach a bus stop", "the decision by some families to withdraw children from school entirely"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about programs designed without local community input?", options: ["They are universally more effective than programs involving parents and village leaders directly.", "They require no funding at all compared to community-designed educational interventions.", "They tend to achieve lower rates of sustained attendance than programs designed with community consultation.", "They are exclusively used in urban areas rather than in remote mountain communities."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Wind Power and the Limits of an Intermittent Resource", paragraphs: ["Wind energy occupies a curious position within the broader landscape of renewable power generation: simultaneously one of the most mature and cost-competitive renewable technologies available today, and one whose fundamental physical characteristics impose constraints that no amount of engineering refinement can fully eliminate. [1] Understanding both dimensions, the genuine technological achievement and the persistent structural limitation, is essential for evaluating wind power's realistic role within a future energy system rather than treating it as either an unambiguous solution or a fundamentally flawed technology.", "The technological maturation of wind power over recent decades has been genuinely remarkable. Modern turbines, towering well over one hundred meters and equipped with blades exceeding sixty meters in length, generate vastly more electricity per unit than their predecessors from even a decade earlier, a consequence of incremental engineering improvements in blade aerodynamics, generator efficiency, and structural materials capable of withstanding greater mechanical stress without proportionally greater cost. [2] These improvements, combined with manufacturing economies of scale as production volumes have increased globally, have driven down the cost of wind-generated electricity to the point where, in many regions with favorable wind conditions, it now competes directly with fossil fuel generation on cost alone, without requiring government subsidy to remain economically viable.", "Despite this cost competitiveness, wind power's defining characteristic, intermittency, remains an unavoidable consequence of the resource itself rather than a solvable engineering deficiency. Wind speed fluctuates continuously according to weather patterns that, while increasingly predictable over short timeframes through improved forecasting models, cannot be controlled or guaranteed in the way that fuel combustion in a conventional power plant can. [3] A wind farm may generate electricity at near-maximum capacity during a windy afternoon and produce almost nothing twelve hours later during a calm evening, precisely the period when household electricity demand often peaks as people return home and begin using lighting, appliances, and heating or cooling systems. This mismatch between generation timing and demand timing represents the central structural challenge that distinguishes wind power from traditional, dispatchable generation sources that can be activated on demand regardless of weather conditions.", "Energy storage technology has emerged as the most widely discussed potential solution to this mismatch, with grid-scale battery installations increasingly deployed alongside wind farms to capture excess electricity generated during high-wind periods for release during subsequent low-wind periods when demand exceeds immediate generation. While battery costs have declined substantially over the past decade, current storage capacity remains insufficient to fully buffer extended periods of low wind across large geographic areas, meaning that batteries currently function more effectively as a short-term smoothing mechanism than as a comprehensive solution capable of eliminating intermittency entirely across multi-day timescales. Some researchers argue that pursuing ever-larger battery installations may ultimately prove less cost-effective than diversifying the broader generation mix to include complementary renewable sources, such as solar power, whose generation patterns do not perfectly correlate with wind availability.", "Grid interconnection across larger geographic areas offers another partial mitigation strategy, since wind conditions affecting one region rarely affect a sufficiently distant region simultaneously, meaning that a sufficiently extensive transmission network can theoretically smooth out localized fluctuations by drawing on wind generation from wherever conditions happen to be favorable at any given moment. This approach, however, requires substantial infrastructure investment in transmission capacity that many existing electrical grids were never originally designed to accommodate, since historical grid architecture generally assumed electricity would be generated relatively close to where it would ultimately be consumed rather than transported across vast distances from geographically dispersed renewable installations.", "Critics of aggressive wind power expansion sometimes invoke intermittency as evidence that wind cannot serve as a primary component of a reliable electrical grid, a position that proponents counter by noting that no electrical grid, including those dominated by fossil fuels, achieves perfect reliability without some form of backup capacity or demand management, and that the relevant comparison should therefore focus on the relative cost and complexity of managing wind's particular variability rather than treating any variability whatsoever as inherently disqualifying. [4] This framing reorients the debate away from whether wind power is reliable in some absolute sense toward the more productive question of what combination of storage, transmission, complementary generation, and demand flexibility can most cost-effectively accommodate wind's genuine, but not necessarily disqualifying, variability.", "Ultimately, wind power's trajectory illustrates a broader pattern increasingly evident across renewable energy technologies: dramatic cost reductions and genuine engineering achievement do not eliminate underlying physical constraints, but rather shift the central challenge from generation cost toward system-level integration, storage, and grid architecture, areas where continued innovation will likely determine how large a role wind power ultimately plays in the broader transition away from fossil fuel generation."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Wind power's intermittency has been completely solved through advances in grid-scale battery storage technology alone.", "Wind power is fundamentally unreliable and should be abandoned in favor of exclusively fossil-fuel-based generation.", "Wind power has become so cost-effective that intermittency no longer poses any meaningful challenge for electrical grids.", "Wind power represents a genuine technological achievement, but its intermittency remains a structural challenge requiring system-level solutions."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has driven down the cost of wind-generated electricity in many regions?", options: ["Government subsidies that have permanently lowered the price of wind-generated electricity in most countries.", "Engineering improvements and manufacturing economies of scale, allowing wind to compete with fossil fuels without subsidy.", "Reduced demand for electricity overall, lowering the price of all energy sources including wind.", "A complete elimination of intermittency, which previously required costly backup generation capacity."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the central structural challenge distinguishing wind power from dispatchable generation sources?", options: ["Wind turbines require significantly more land area than any other form of renewable energy generation.", "A mismatch between when wind generates electricity and when household electricity demand actually peaks.", "Wind power cannot be transmitted across any distance without substantial loss of electrical energy.", "Wind turbines produce more pollution during manufacturing than equivalent fossil fuel power plants."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why might diversifying the generation mix be more cost-effective than relying solely on ever-larger battery installations?", options: ["Diversifying generation eliminates the need for any transmission infrastructure investment whatsoever.", "Battery technology has become too expensive to deploy at any scale alongside wind farms.", "Solar power generates significantly more total electricity than wind power in nearly all regions studied.", "Complementary renewable sources like solar have generation patterns that do not perfectly correlate with wind availability."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The passage discusses grid interconnection as a mitigation strategy. Which of the following is also true in the passage but does NOT directly explain why interconnection helps smooth fluctuations?", options: ["Wind conditions affecting one region rarely affect a sufficiently distant region simultaneously.", "Historical grid architecture generally assumed electricity would be generated close to where it is consumed.", "Modern turbines generate vastly more electricity per unit than their predecessors from a decade earlier.", "A sufficiently extensive transmission network can draw on generation from wherever conditions are favorable."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following statements, while true according to the passage, does NOT directly support the proponents' counterargument about reliability?", options: ["Grid-scale battery installations have become increasingly deployed alongside wind farms in recent years.", "No electrical grid achieves perfect reliability without some form of backup capacity or demand management.", "Critics sometimes invoke intermittency as evidence that wind cannot serve as a primary grid component.", "The relevant comparison should focus on the relative cost and complexity of managing variability."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"dispatchable\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["permanently disconnected from the electrical grid", "able to be activated on demand regardless of external conditions", "dependent entirely on unpredictable weather patterns", "incapable of generating electricity under any circumstances"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"reorients\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["financially weakens", "physically constructs", "permanently eliminates", "redirects or shifts in focus"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"this\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["the strategy of expanding transmission networks across larger geographic areas", "the historical assumption that electricity is generated close to consumption", "the decision by researchers to diversify the renewable generation mix", "the practice of deploying grid-scale battery installations alongside wind farms"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Proponents argue that framing the debate this way leads to more productive policy discussions than simply dismissing wind power outright.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
];
